Node.js with the base-system LibreSSL
=====================================

Maintainer notes.  This file is not installed; user-facing notes would go
in `pkg/README`, which would change the package file list.

This port builds Node.js v24.22.2 against the LibreSSL shipped in the
OpenBSD base system.  It no longer depends on security/openssl and does
not install or link a private copy of OpenSSL.

Why patches are required
------------------------

Node.js upstream supports OpenSSL 3 and BoringSSL.  LibreSSL implements
most of the OpenSSL 1.1 API but not the OpenSSL 3 provider API.  Node's
`deps/ncrypto` library has a legacy OpenSSL backend that is selected when
`OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER` is below 3.0.0 (LibreSSL reports 0x20000000L), so
the provider, OSSL_PARAM, EVP_MAC and EVP_KDF paths are not compiled.
The remaining incompatibilities are legacy interfaces that LibreSSL does
not provide.

The patches in `patches/` add a small compatibility header and guard the
affected call sites.  They do not emulate providers, do not weaken
certificate validation, and do not disable TLS or cryptographic checks.

Compatibility summary
---------------------

Validated on Linux before the final TLS retry patch:
  - hashes, HMAC, AES-CBC/CTR/GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305
  - RSA, RSA-PSS, ECDSA, Ed25519, X25519, DH and DSA key operations
  - PBKDF2, HKDF, random bytes, key import/export
  - X.509 parsing, chain verification, hostname and IP checks
  - TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 client and server handshakes, ALPN
  - WebCrypto for the algorithms above
  - rejection of self-signed chains and explicit CA acceptance

Partially validated:
  - SNI contexts and ALPN use the LibreSSL context path.  Asynchronous
    `SNICallback`, `OCSPRequest` and `cert` functions are rejected.
  - A TLS 1.3 client that writes from the same tick as `secureConnect`
    exposed a write-ordering problem in the Linux build: the first
    application record could be queued before pending handshake data was
    consumed.  The patch series adds a retry after draining that data,
    but this revision has not been built or retested since the change.
    HTTPS therefore remains pending validation on OpenBSD.

Not available with LibreSSL:
  - OpenSSL 3 providers, FIPS mode and property queries
  - scrypt (LibreSSL has no scrypt implementation)
  - AES-OCB, SM2, Ed448, X448
  - multi-prime RSA keys
  - XOF digests (cSHAKE, TurboSHAKE, KT128/KT256)
  - KMAC, GMAC, Argon2
  - `tls.setSigalgs()`, `tlsSocket.getSharedSigalgs()`
  - asynchronous certificate/OCSP callbacks (`SSL_set_cert_cb`)
  - early ClientHello session lookup used by server `resumeSession`
  - raw TLS ticket access (`tlsSocket.getTLSTicket()`)
  - the OpenSSL secure heap (`--secure-heap` fails closed)

The `pskCallback` option is rejected because LibreSSL is built with
`OPENSSL_NO_PSK`; it is not silently ignored.

Build notes
-----------

The port keeps `--shared-openssl` but uses the base include and library
paths.  `WANTLIB` lists plain `crypto` and `ssl`.  No rpath is needed.
Tests use `/usr/bin/openssl` from the base system.

`distinfo` and `pkg/PLIST` are the official copies because the version and
installed file set are unchanged.  Regenerate them on OpenBSD after any
version or file-set change:
  - `distinfo`
  - `pkg/PLIST`
  - any generated `*.inc` file

The 52 unmodified official patches retain the official OpenBSD style.  Run
the patch style checker on the added or modified LibreSSL patches only; all
of those pass.

Linux development build
-----------------------

A LibreSSL build can be reproduced outside OpenBSD with Homebrew
LibreSSL.  Use a private prefix and do not modify the system Node.js:

    L=$(brew --prefix libressl)
    LDFLAGS="-Wl,-rpath,$L/lib" ./configure \
      --prefix="$HOME/node-libressl" \
      --shared-openssl \
      --shared-openssl-includes="$L/include" \
      --shared-openssl-libpath="$L/lib"
    make -j"$(nproc)"
    ldd out/Release/node | grep -E 'libssl|libcrypto'

Validate against the base system LibreSSL on OpenBSD with the normal
ports targets:

    make clean=all
    make patch
    make configure
    make build
    make test

Runtime and crypto checks must be repeated on OpenBSD; Linux results do
not prove pledge/unveil, W^X, libc++ or base-LibreSSL behaviour.
